These are foundational terms in IAM / ABAC discussions. Interviewers expect you to explain them clearly, simply, and confidently—not with vendor marketing language.
Below is the bank-grade explanation you can use.

1️⃣ IGA — Identity Governance & Administration
What it is
IGA is about governing and controlling access over time.
	Think: Who should have access, why, for how long, and who approved it?
What IGA does
	• Joiner / mover / leaver access
	• Access requests & approvals
	• Periodic access reviews (recertification)
	• Segregation of Duties (SoD)
	• Audit & compliance reporting
Role in ABAC
	• IGA does NOT make runtime access decisions
	• It manages entitlements that become attributes for ABAC
One-liner (interview gold)
	IGA governs access lifecycle; ABAC enforces access at runtime.

2️⃣ AIP — Azure Information Protection
What it is
AIP classifies, labels, and protects data based on sensitivity.
	Think: What is the data and how sensitive is it?
What AIP provides
	• Data classification labels (Public, Confidential, Highly Confidential)
	• Encryption
	• Usage restrictions (no copy, no forward, no print)
	• Persistent protection (data stays protected even outside the org)
Role in ABAC
	• AIP labels become data attributes
	• ABAC policies use these attributes for access decisions
Example
	“Only users with clearance ≥ Confidential can access data labeled Confidential.”
One-liner
	AIP defines data sensitivity; ABAC uses it to control access.

3️⃣ Data Catalogue
What it is
A Data Catalogue is a centralized inventory of enterprise data assets.
	Think: What data do we have, where is it, who owns it, and how sensitive is it?
What it contains
	• Dataset metadata
	• Data owner
	• Classification
	• Residency / jurisdiction
	• Business context
Role in ABAC
	• Provides resource attributes
	• Enables policies like:
		○ Ownership-based access
		○ Jurisdiction-based access
		○ Sensitivity-based access
One-liner
	A data catalogue makes data discoverable and governable.

4️⃣ IdP — Identity Provider
What it is
An IdP authenticates users and issues identity assertions.
	Think: Who is the user and can they prove it?
What an IdP does
	• User authentication
	• Issues tokens (SAML / OAuth / OIDC)
	• Provides identity attributes as claims
	• Supports MFA, Conditional Access
Examples
	• On-prem Active Directory
	• Azure Entra ID
Role in ABAC
	• Supplies identity and context attributes
	• Does NOT decide authorization
One-liner
	IdP proves identity; ABAC decides access.

5️⃣ How They Work Together (This Is the Big Picture)
End-to-End Flow
	1. IdP authenticates the user and provides identity attributes
	2. IGA supplies business entitlements
	3. AIP / Data Catalogue provide data sensitivity and metadata
	4. ABAC engine evaluates policies
	5. Application enforces the decision

6️⃣ 15-Second Summary (Memorize This)
	IdP authenticates users, IGA governs who should have access, AIP and data catalogues define data sensitivity, and ABAC brings everything together to make dynamic, policy-based access decisions.

